   old=r"""The contribution of this thesis is positioned at the intersection of the four identified gaps, and its architecture follows from that positioning. The analytical artefact is the Tri-Lateral Retention Feasibility Model, a constraint formulation in the tradition of formal legal-compliance verification but in the modelling style of operations research. It decomposes each cross-border audit record into event metadata and a committed personal-data payload. It expresses the audit-retention floors, the Article 17 erasure right, and the Article 68(12) deletion ceiling as constraints over discretised time, and examines joint satisfiability under two semantics of audit verifiability. The model is tri-lateral in structure rather than in instrument count: the floor is parameterised, so that the ten-year eHDSI cross-border audit floor and the one-year Article 73(1)(e) Secure Processing Environment log floor are two instances of a single constraint, and jurisdiction-specific floors can be substituted without reconstruction. Under the prevailing plaintext semantics the system is infeasible; under verifiability-as-accountability-preservation it is jointly satisfiable. The mechanism that witnesses this feasibility, Crypto-Erasure by Invalidation, applies the NIST-recognised cryptographic-erase technique at the audit-trail boundary. It operates within the regulatory corridor defined by the EDPB's unrecoverability test and the Norwegian anonymisation-equals-deletion doctrine, with hiding commitments and the invalidation registry supplying the verifiability that the practitioner crypto-shredding pattern lacks (accumulator witnesses being a specified but unimplemented optional layer).""",
